<clickhouse>
    <!--
      Memory configuration for a container-limited ClickHouse.

      THE ROOT CAUSE, found the long way round.

      ClickHouse's default cache limits are sized for a dedicated server: 5 GiB of marks, 5 GiB
      of index marks and 8 GiB of uncompressed blocks - 18 GiB of cache ceilings on a container
      limited to 6 GiB. Those caches are counted by the server's memory tracker, which is why the
      tracker kept reporting figures that had no relationship to what the process was actually
      using: 5.40 GiB committed against 1.15 GiB RSS, and later 4.51 GiB against 2.15 GiB.

      That single misconfiguration produced every memory symptom this project chased:

        * queries killed non-deterministically by the OvercommitTracker while the container had
          gigabytes free (the tracker was full of cache, the machine was not);
        * a workaround setting max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio to 4, which stopped the
          killing by telling the server it could use four times the VM's RAM - so it never
          self-limited, and background merges after the daily-partitioning migration grew until
          the container thrashed and `SELECT 1` timed out for minutes;
        * and then, after replacing the ratio with a hard ceiling, queries being killed again -
          because the caches were still allowed to fill 18 GiB of that ceiling.

      Sizing the caches to the container fixes the cause rather than the symptom. The tracker
      then reports something close to reality, the hard ceiling becomes meaningful, and per-query
      limits do what they say.

      IN PRODUCTION, scale all four numbers with the memory the container is actually given -
      roughly 10-15% of it for marks, and the server ceiling at 70-80%.
    -->

    <!--
      5.2 GiB against a 6 GiB container.

      This was first set to 4.5 GiB, which was too tight once background merges ran alongside a
      job: the per-query limits held, each aggregate stayed under 1 GiB, and the server total
      still reached the ceiling and the OvercommitTracker started stopping queries. 4.5 was
      chosen while the caches could still claim 18 GiB; with them bounded to well under a
      gigabyte, the remaining headroom is real and the ceiling can sit closer to the container
      limit.
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>5583457484</max_server_memory_usage>

    <!--
      Marks are the primary index in memory: keeping them cached is what makes a point lookup
      read 278K rows out of a billion in 12 ms. 512 MiB is ample for 25 GiB of data with a
      sparse index, and it is a bound rather than a reservation.
    -->
    <mark_cache_size>536870912</mark_cache_size>
    <index_mark_cache_size>268435456</index_mark_cache_size>

    <!--
      The uncompressed cache is off by the per-query default (use_uncompressed_cache = 0) and
      helps only workloads that re-read the same small ranges repeatedly. This one does not: the
      marts scan wide and the dashboard reads the marts. An 8 GiB ceiling for a cache nothing
      asks for is 8 GiB of tracked memory for nothing.
    -->
    <uncompressed_cache_size>134217728</uncompressed_cache_size>
    <!--
      MERGE SIZE, capped to what this container can actually afford.

      Section 2 of docs/architecture/11-clickhouse-memory.md records that a single merge of a
      multi-gigabyte part holds around 4 GiB here, and that merging a 6.45 GiB table took the
      server down entirely. It was written as something to work around - batch jobs pause merges,
      the IMSI backfill waits for the drain - and never as something to prevent.

      Working around it is not enough, because the one moment nothing can pause merges is
      STARTUP. After an unclean stop the server wakes with a merge backlog, schedules the largest
      one it can, exhausts the VM, is killed, restarts, and schedules it again. Observed directly:
      three restart cycles in four minutes, the host down to 2.6 GiB free, and the Docker engine
      itself returning 500 because the VM had nothing left to give it.

      The default ceiling on a single merge is 150 GiB, which assumes a machine that has it.
      2 GiB is what this one has to spare, so that is the cap.

      What it costs: large tables stop merging once their parts reach 2 GiB, so binding_current
      (6.65 GiB) settles at roughly four parts rather than one. That is not a regression - four
      parts is inside the range measured as cheap for FINAL, and the alternative was a merge that
      takes the server with it. Correctness is unaffected: ReplacingMergeTree is resolved by
      FINAL at query time, never by having merged.

      IN PRODUCTION, raise this with the container's memory. It is a statement about available
      RAM, not about the data.
    -->
    <merge_tree>
        <max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool>2147483648</max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool>
        <!--
          Without this, ClickHouse lowers the cap further as the pool fills, using the value above
          as the maximum. Zero means the cap above is the cap, whatever the pool is doing.
        -->
        <number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_lower_max_size_of_merge>0</number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_lower_max_size_of_merge>

        <!--
          Lowered from 20 because the pool below is smaller than the one that default assumes.
          ClickHouse refuses to start if this exceeds background_pool_size x
          background_merges_mutations_concurrency_ratio - a good check, and the one that caught
          the first version of this file: pool 2 x ratio 2 = 4, against a required 20, and the
          server exited with BAD_ARGUMENTS on every restart. There are two such guards, and
          they are checked one at a time, so fixing the first only reveals the second.
        -->
        <number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation>2</number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation>
        <number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_optimize_entire_partition>2</number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_optimize_entire_partition>
    </merge_tree>

    <!--
      Four merge threads and no task multiplier, so at most four merges run at once. The cap above
      bounds what one merge holds; this bounds how many can hold it simultaneously, which is the
      other half of the same arithmetic. The default is 16 threads with a ratio of 2 - thirty-two
      concurrent tasks, which is sizing for a machine that has thirty-two cores and the RAM to
      match.
    -->
    <background_pool_size>4</background_pool_size>
    <background_merges_mutations_concurrency_ratio>1</background_merges_mutations_concurrency_ratio>
</clickhouse>
